            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"UNPERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"NONE\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book entry defines the 'lost sheep' (specifically of the house of Israel, citing Matt 15:24) as the scattered house mingled with the nations. However, the exact phrase 'lost sheep of the house of Israel' is used in Matthew 10:5-6 and Matthew 15:24 to explicitly exclude the Gentiles and nations. In Matthew 10, Jesus commands the disciples, 'Go not into the way of the Gentiles... But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.' In Matthew 15:24, Jesus uses the phrase to initially decline help to a Canaanite woman, distinguishing the 'lost sheep' (the children) from the nations. If the lost sheep were the scattered tribes assimilated into the nations (which the chapter identifies as the 'fulness of the Gentiles'), Jesus' contrast in both passages would contradict His own geographic and ethnic restrictions. The counter-interpretation—that the 'lost sheep of the house of Israel' refers to the ethnic Jews of Jesus' day, while 'lost sheep' in general means any straying sinner—better explains the biblical usage without contradiction.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The primary referent of 'lost sheep' is the scattered house of Israel mingled with the nations, and secondarily any unrepentant sinner.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"sought and regathered\",\n    \"more generally, any unrepentant sinner\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [\n    \"The scattered house of Israel mingled with the nations\"\n  ],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's identification of Ephraim as the 'fulness of the Gentiles' (Romans 11:25) conflates the scattering of the northern kingdom with Paul's theology of ethnic Gentile salvation.\",\n    \"The chapter equates the two sticks of Ezekiel 37 with Judah and a scattered, Gentile-assimilated Ephraim.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The 'lost sheep' is a metaphor for a sinner who has gone astray (Luke 15), and the specific phrase 'lost sheep of the house of Israel' refers to the ethnic covenant people of Israel to whom Jesus restricted His earthly ministry, explicitly distinguishing them from the Gentiles and Samaritans.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A straying sinner (generally) and the ethnic Jewish people of Jesus' earthly ministry (specifically).\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The 'lost sheep of the house of Israel' are not those mingled with the nations, but are explicitly contrasted with the Gentiles and nations in the passages where the phrase appears.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"In Matthew 10:5-6, Jesus tells the twelve: 'Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.' This explicitly separates the 'lost sheep' from the nations/Gentiles.\",\n    \"In Matthew 15:22-26, Jesus tells a Canaanite woman he was sent only to the 'lost sheep of the house of Israel,' contrasting this group (the 'children') with her (representing the non-Israelite nations).\",\n    \"If the 'lost sheep' were the scattered house mingled with the nations, instructing disciples to avoid the Gentiles in order to reach them would be a logical impossibility.\",\n    \"Therefore, the 'lost sheep of the house of Israel' refers to the recognized Jewish population of the first century, while the general metaphor of a 'lost sheep' applies to any straying sinner.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Connects the concept of being 'lost' to the historical scattering of the northern tribes (Ephraim) among the nations.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Explains the explicit geographical and ethnic restrictions in Matthew 10 and Matthew 15.\",\n    \"Respects the textual contrast between the 'lost sheep of the house of Israel' and the Gentiles/nations.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [\n    \"Matthew 10:5-6, where Jesus forbids going to the Gentiles in order to go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.\",\n    \"Matthew 15:24-26, where the 'lost sheep' status is used to deny immediate ministry to a woman from the nations.\"\n  ],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"COUNTER\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Matthew 10:5-6\",\n    \"Matthew 15:24\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
